{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "sentences-everyday",
  "title": "Everyday Sentences",
  "description": "Short sentences from the first words: wanting, having, where things are, and saying no. Notice what's missing: no 'is', no 'a' or 'the', and verbs that never change.",
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "s-01",
      "indonesian": "Saya mau makan.",
      "english": "I want to eat.",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "t": "Saya ",
          "w": "w-saya"
        },
        {
          "t": "mau ",
          "w": "w-mau"
        },
        {
          "t": "makan.",
          "w": "w-makan"
        }
      ],
      "registerNotes": "Mau goes straight before the verb, with no word for 'to'. It's the everyday 'want' or 'would like', polite enough for anyone.",
      "audio": "audio/id/sentences/s-01.mp3",
      "tags": [
        "wants",
        "food"
      ],
      "difficulty": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "s-02",
      "indonesian": "Ini rumah saya.",
      "english": "This is my house.",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "t": "Ini ",
          "w": "w-ini"
        },
        {
          "t": "rumah ",
          "w": "w-rumah"
        },
        {
          "t": "saya.",
          "w": "w-saya"
        }
      ],
      "pitfallNote": "There's no 'is', and the owner comes after the thing: rumah saya is 'my house'.",
      "audio": "audio/id/sentences/s-02.mp3",
      "tags": [
        "home",
        "this and that"
      ],
      "difficulty": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "s-03",
      "indonesian": "Dia tidak ada di rumah.",
      "english": "She's not at home.",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "t": "Dia ",
          "w": "w-dia"
        },
        {
          "t": "tidak ",
          "w": "w-tidak"
        },
        {
          "t": "ada ",
          "w": "w-ada"
        },
        {
          "t": "di rumah.",
          "w": "w-rumah"
        }
      ],
      "registerNotes": "What you'll hear when you call for someone who's out. Dia is he or she, so this could just as well be 'He's not at home'.",
      "audio": "audio/id/sentences/s-03.mp3",
      "tags": [
        "people",
        "ada"
      ],
      "difficulty": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "s-04",
      "indonesian": "Kamu tinggal di mana?",
      "english": "Where do you live?",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "t": "Kamu ",
          "w": "w-kamu"
        },
        {
          "t": "tinggal ",
          "w": "w-tinggal"
        },
        {
          "t": "di mana?",
          "w": "w-di-mana"
        }
      ],
      "registerNotes": "Kamu is for someone your age or younger. To an older person, put Bapak or Ibu in its place: Ibu tinggal di mana?",
      "pitfallNote": "No 'do' and no change of order: the question word sits where the answer goes, as in Saya tinggal di Jakarta.",
      "audio": "audio/id/sentences/s-04.mp3",
      "tags": [
        "questions",
        "small talk"
      ],
      "difficulty": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "s-05",
      "indonesian": "Ini teman saya, Rina.",
      "english": "This is my friend Rina.",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "t": "Ini ",
          "w": "w-ini"
        },
        {
          "t": "teman ",
          "w": "w-teman"
        },
        {
          "t": "saya, ",
          "w": "w-saya"
        },
        {
          "t": "Rina."
        }
      ],
      "registerNotes": "How you introduce someone. Swap in suami (husband), istri (wife) or pacar (partner): Ini suami saya, Tom.",
      "audio": "audio/id/sentences/s-05.mp3",
      "tags": [
        "people",
        "introductions"
      ],
      "difficulty": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "s-06",
      "indonesian": "Saya sudah makan.",
      "english": "I've already eaten.",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "t": "Saya ",
          "w": "w-saya"
        },
        {
          "t": "sudah ",
          "w": "w-sudah"
        },
        {
          "t": "makan.",
          "w": "w-makan"
        }
      ],
      "pitfallNote": "Makan doesn't change for the past: sudah (already) does that job.",
      "cultureNote": "The usual answer to Sudah makan?, a friendly greeting rather than an invitation. If you haven't, the answer is Belum (not yet).",
      "audio": "audio/id/sentences/s-06.mp3",
      "tags": [
        "food",
        "time words"
      ],
      "difficulty": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "s-07",
      "indonesian": "Itu bukan tas saya.",
      "english": "That's not my bag.",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "t": "Itu ",
          "w": "w-itu"
        },
        {
          "t": "bukan ",
          "w": "w-bukan"
        },
        {
          "t": "tas ",
          "w": "w-tas"
        },
        {
          "t": "saya.",
          "w": "w-saya"
        }
      ],
      "pitfallNote": "Bukan, not tidak, before a noun: Itu bukan tas saya, but Saya tidak tahu.",
      "audio": "audio/id/sentences/s-07.mp3",
      "tags": [
        "saying no",
        "things"
      ],
      "difficulty": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "s-08",
      "indonesian": "Saya punya dua anak.",
      "english": "I've got two children.",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "t": "Saya ",
          "w": "w-saya"
        },
        {
          "t": "punya "
        },
        {
          "t": "dua "
        },
        {
          "t": "anak.",
          "w": "w-anak"
        }
      ],
      "pitfallNote": "No plural after a number: dua anak, not dua anak-anak. The number goes first, as in English.",
